WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.970
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.740
SERGEI SOKOLENKO:
Hello and welcome.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.360
My name is Sergei Sokolenko.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.940
I am a product manager
at the Google Cloud.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.580
And today with me, I
have George Hilios,

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.500
vice president of Two Sigma.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.800
Talking to you about advancing
serverless data processing

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:25.110
in Cloud Dataflow by separating
state and compute storage.

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:28.950
But before we talk about
separating compute and sate

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:32.369
storage, I wanted
to do very quickly,

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:34.710
a historical
retrospective of how

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.600
the industry used to
process really largely data

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.760
sets in the past.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.820
Imagine it's the
year 2000, and you

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:47.090
have a one petabyte large data
set that you want to process.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:47.900
Who am I kidding?

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:50.350
In the year 2000, a
really large data set

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:53.530
was probably something
you measured in terabytes.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.180
Well, still, you have
a terabyte of data.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.650
And you want to process
it, and it's the year 2000.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.600
So you'd probably use something
like this, an architecture,

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.470
something like this.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:07.150
You would use a large
box with lots of CPU

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:11.430
and might be a
mainframe or a Unix box.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:18.040
The storage would be provided
through a storage area network.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.560
Then come 2004, new distributed
processing frameworks

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.090
were released.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:28.200
And now, you would probably be
using a cluster of cheap Linux

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.010
nodes with storage actually
being part of that processing

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.540
node, so local storage.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.840
Then as things moved
into the cloud,

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.560
you were still using these
clusters of Linux nodes.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.320
But now, you would be using
network-attached storage

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.540
provided by the cloud provider.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.130
In 2010, Google
published a white paper.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:50.970
We called it the
Dremel paper, which

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.940
defined the new architecture
for processing really

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.790
large web-scale data sets.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:02:00.360
And that architecture was
based on the following idea.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.320
You would have a independent
highly-available cluster

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.370
of compute.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:09.690
And you would have
highly-reliable storage

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.600
facilities.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.300
In 2011, we launched
a product that

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.380
was based on this white
paper, on this concept.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:18.500
We called it BigQuery.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.240
And probably at this
conference, you've

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.480
heard about BigQuery many times.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.830
Became a very
successful product.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.520
So this is the
architecture of BigQuery.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:32.690
It has the concepts described
in the Dremel white people.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:36.020
It has a replicated
storage layer.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:39.380
It has a highly-available
cluster of compute.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:40.370
They're separate.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:41.660
They're working together.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:45.050
And they're connected through
a very fast petabyte scale

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:46.760
network.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:50.120
But there's a component in this
architecture that was not part

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.540
of the original Dremel paper.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.860
It's the [INAUDIBLE]
in-memory Shuffle [INAUDIBLE]..

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.180
Now you might ask yourself
what is this Shuffle?

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:03.490
Why was it added later
on when we actually

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:07.610
launched the product into
general availability?

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:10.510
So let's just quickly cover
the concept of Shuffle.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:13.610
With Shuffle in data
processing, when

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:17.490
you have a big collection
of key value pairs,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:21.620
and you want to do a grouping
or a join with another data set,

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.150
you need to sort these elements.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.290
And when you have a single
node processing architecture,

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.730
you can easily sort
it more or less.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:33.470
Either in-memory, you can sort
your data set in memory by key.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:38.330
Or you have efficient
on-disk sorting algorithms.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:41.350
Unfortunately, once
your data sets explode,

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:43.610
and you have to move
to a distributed

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.570
framework or a
distributed architecture

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:50.370
where you have lots of nodes, it
becomes somewhat more complex.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.420
So your end goal is
to have everything

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:57.710
sorted by key and all
data elements associated

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:03.820
with a particular key co-located
on a particular worker node.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:05.860
And what you end up
doing is you end up

00:04:05.860 --> 00:04:09.940
shuffling or physically moving
data elements associated

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:13.150
with specific keys from
one box to another.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.890
And that's the
process of shuffling.

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:18.380
Now as you can
imagine, this is a very

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.680
resource-intensive and
time-intensive process.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:27.080
And as your data sets scale, it
becomes more and more complex.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.960
That's why BigQuery
ended up building

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.360
a separate dedicated
component that

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.020
was just responsible for
shuffling big data sets.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:39.740
And it became a
critical component

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:46.950
that connected the storage
as well as the compute nodes.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:48.630
Now I am a product
manager of Dataflow,

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:50.560
and this is a
Dataflow-related question.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:53.340
So you might ask yourself,
why am I spending so much time

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:54.820
talking about Shuffle?

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:57.390
We actually, in Dataflow
have the same problem.

00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:02.940
We are providing the same data
transformations to customers.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:06.710
We allow them to do groupings,
and joins, and aggregations,

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:08.580
and filtering, and projections.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.340
So the problem set is
actually quite similar to what

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.610
BigQuery is dealing with.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:17.150
Here's a screenshot of a
real Dataflow pipeline.

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:22.150
Let's just follow this pipeline
as it lives in our service.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:25.660
Simplistically, it can be
presented by this diagram.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:28.060
We have a couple
of data sources.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:29.920
We have data transformations.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.600
That's the green
things over there.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:35.650
And then we have joined
some group [INAUDIBLE]..

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:38.620
That's a special kind of
a data transformation.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:43.240
And finally, the data
ends up in a data sync.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:45.540
So as you submit
this Dataflow job

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:48.350
to our service,
Cloud Dataflow, we

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:50.880
are going to create a cluster
of nodes to process it.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.570
And these will be Compute
Engine virtual machines,

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:59.500
and we'll also use
persistent disk storage.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:01.500
And they're going to start
reading from sources.

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:07.650
Once we have the green boxes,
the data transformations,

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:10.890
they're going to
run them on compute

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:13.800
and use some local storage.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:17.030
And then when things enter the
joins and the group [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:19.470
they're actually
going to do a Shuffle.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:23.490
And we're going to do the
Shuffle today using resistance

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:24.560
storage--

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:28.020
either the magnetic
type or SSD type,

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.900
whatever the customer specifies.

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.840
Ultimately, data gets
written into sync,

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:34.270
and we shut down the cluster.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:35.850
And that's kind
of, in a nutshell,

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:39.140
how a Dataflow job works.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:41.390
Now in Dataflow,
as I said, we are

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:45.470
dealing with the same kinds of
volume and scalability issues

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:47.730
that BigQuery is dealing with.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:51.680
We have huge volumes of data
that customers want to process.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:55.430
And so we ended up implementing
a very similar mechanism

00:06:55.430 --> 00:07:00.140
for a distributed in-memory
Shuffle service in Dataflow.

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:02.450
And the service works like this.

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:05.910
You have your compute clusters
and data on your user code,

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:09.080
which you write either
in Java or Python.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:10.860
Then we have the
Dataflow Shuffle.

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:13.160
And the Dataflow
Shuffle is deployed

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:20.460
for high-availability reasons
into multiple GCP regions.

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:23.910
So in each GCP region where a
Dataflow Shuffle is available,

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.950
we also have replication
and duplication

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:30.080
of Shuffle in every zone.

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:32.180
We have it deployed
in every zone.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.640
We have a component we call
the outer zone placement.

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:36.800
And this component
decides, based

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:40.460
on the available capacity
and the job, which zone

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.360
it needs to be assigned to.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:46.610
So we take care of
deciding which zone to run.

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:49.790
And then we have a
tier of components

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:52.470
which coordinate
the actual Shuffle

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.690
operation among themselves.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:57.560
We have a Shuffle proxy,
which accepts the job,

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:03.960
and we have two file systems.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.820
One is in-memory, and
the other one is on-disk.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:10.910
And so if your job and
the available capacity

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:14.810
allows us to do a Shuffle
entirely in memory,

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:17.330
your data will never touch
disk and will quickly

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:19.130
join results to you.

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:22.460
But if your job is
size such as that we

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:25.250
have to cache some
of this data on disk,

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:30.200
we're going to transparently
move some of your data

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:32.880
from in-memory into disk
and then Shuffle it there.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:34.880
For you, you don't have
to worry about anything.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:39.600
We do transparent shuffling, and
we just return results to you.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:42.419
So this entire architecture
requires no code changes

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:43.799
from users.

00:08:43.799 --> 00:08:46.720
You can tell us just by
specifying a parameter

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:49.930
that you want to use
the Dataflow Shuffle.

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:54.720
And we're going to switch
from the PD-based Shuffle--

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:57.480
the one that we discussed
earlier in the talk--

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:01.820
to a service-based Shuffle
transparently to you.

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:03.990
Now today, I'm very
happy to announce

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:08.030
general availability of Dataflow
Shuffle in two GCP regions--

00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:11.260
in US central one
and US west one.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:14.130
Dataflow Shuffle has
been in beta for awhile.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:18.910
Now, it's available
generally to all our users.

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:23.400
Let's quickly go through all the
benefits of Dataflow Shuffle.

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.340
The first benefit is
many of our customers

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:31.320
tell us that Shuffle is now
much faster than it used to be.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:36.380
So if we take the same pipeline
and compare the execution times

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:41.930
of this pipeline using
magnetic disks, magnetic PD,

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:47.630
then SSD-PD, and the Dataflow
Shuffle-- the Shuffle service--

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:50.510
you're going to see that
the magnetic disks give you

00:09:50.510 --> 00:09:54.806
maybe 55 minutes of duration
in your pipeline execution.

00:09:54.806 --> 00:09:56.180
Now [INAUDIBLE]
pipeline, the one

00:09:56.180 --> 00:10:01.450
that is using SSD-PDs,
persist in disks,

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:05.030
will run in
approximately 17 minutes.

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:10.490
The Dataflow Shuffle one will
actually run in 10 minutes.

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:13.310
These results are not always
applicable to every use case,

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:15.740
but many of our
customers are telling us

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:18.550
that's what they are seeing.

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:20.800
The other benefit
of Dataflow Shuffle

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:23.170
is that we are now able
to process and shuffle

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.490
much larger data sets.

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:27.020
And you're going
to see in a demo

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:31.080
the sizes of shuffles
we can now support.

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:35.650
If previously the untuned jobs--
the ones that used magnetic

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:36.940
disks--

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:40.510
were able to shuffle tens
of terabytes of data,

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:45.700
and jobs that used SSD-PDs
were able to shuffle up

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:48.540
to 50 terabytes of
data, now, we can push

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.368
into hundreds of terabytes.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:54.540
So with this, I
wanted to show you

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:58.330
a demo that actually
runs a Shuffle job.

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:00.180
And here's how the
job looks like.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:01.290
I have two inputs--

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:05.820
two GCS buckets, a data
set and GCS buckets.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:09.150
And I'm going to read a 50
terabyte data set in each case,

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:11.090
in each of the inputs.

00:11:11.090 --> 00:11:17.300
And I'm going to join them and
write them into a GCS bucket.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:20.580
The code for this pipeline
is written in Scala

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:24.840
using a framework that one
of our customers developed.

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:26.220
It's called [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:30.600
Spotify are the original
developers of this framework.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:32.100
And I like
[INAUDIBLE],, because it

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:36.090
allows me to very easily
define my pipelines.

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:41.220
The entire pipeline is these
10 to 15 lines of code.

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:43.440
For those of you who
might not see it,

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:46.330
let me just quickly
explain what it does.

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:49.970
In the main function,
I define my first input

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:51.090
and my second input.

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:53.730
And then I do a left outer join.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:56.430
And I write the output to files.

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:58.560
That's all this pipeline does.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:02.550
And let me switch to
the demonstration.

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:04.170
Before we switch
to demonstration,

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:08.910
you might ask me yourself,
how does the input work?

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:11.730
Here's a screenshot
of one of the files

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:13.800
that I will be joining.

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:16.780
It consists of three columns.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:18.700
The first column is the key.

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:20.730
That's the key I
want to join on.

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:24.150
And it's just random
generated strings.

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:26.470
Then I have a record ID.

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:29.150
I have billions of
records in my data set.

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:30.960
And so this record
ID is pretty long.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:33.750
It's a very long string.

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:35.910
And then I have the
payload, the value that I

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:37.960
want to associate with the key.

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:40.350
And so with this, let's
switch to the demonstration,

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:42.060
and let me run a few commands.

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:50.270
Great.

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:55.484
So I'm in bash, in terminal.

00:12:55.484 --> 00:12:56.900
The first thing I
want to show you

00:12:56.900 --> 00:12:59.480
is the contents of
my bucket from which

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:01.580
I will be reading files.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:05.720
I've defined a bash
variable, input 50 TBs.

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:08.480
And this is the
bucket that contains

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:11.040
50 terabytes worth of files.

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:15.620
So I have 50,000
1-gigabyte large files

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:19.550
that have data with
random keys that I

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:21.250
will be joining together.

00:13:21.250 --> 00:13:26.230
And just to show you that this
bucket really contains 50 TBs,

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:29.400
I ran a listing command.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:33.710
And here's my proof it does
really contain 50 terabytes.

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:36.120
I'm not going to run
now, because if I ran it,

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:39.906
then listing 50,000 files will
actually take several minutes.

00:13:39.906 --> 00:13:41.030
That's why I run it before.

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:48.310
Now my next command is the one
that will create the pipeline.

00:13:48.310 --> 00:13:52.540
And I'm going to be using the
Scala build tool to quickly

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:56.140
execute from the command
line the command that

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:58.090
launches a Dataflow job.

00:13:58.090 --> 00:14:00.430
And as you can see, what
I need to specify here

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:03.640
is, what is my input?

00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:09.040
And both of the inputs will be
reading from the sync bucket,

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.490
the output bucket,
and the parameter

00:14:12.490 --> 00:14:15.460
instruction, the
Dataflow service

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:18.160
to use the Shuffle service.

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:22.220
So I'm going to
run this command.

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:31.450
And so within a minute or two,
the code will be packaged.

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:34.060
All of the dependencies
will be deployed.

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:37.560
And I'm going to
initiate my job.

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:40.250
I'm going to give
it a few seconds.

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:42.960
Depending on the Wi-Fi
gods, if we're lucky,

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:48.610
it will be done quickly.

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:50.800
But in any case, I
also ran a Shuffle just

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:57.730
before the demo, a Shuffle
that processed 100 terabytes.

00:14:57.730 --> 00:14:59.730
So here's my job.

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:04.320
Just to give you a few
data points about this job,

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:12.470
the job took 500 billion
elements in my data set.

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:15.230
It was about 51 terabytes.

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:23.210
In the second input, I had also
500 billion rows, another 51.

00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:26.380
And then I joined them
in this operation.

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:31.500
In Dataflow, we call
joins co-group by keys.

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:36.330
And so it created 100, I
think, billion combinations

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:38.010
of these keys.

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:39.390
Some of the keys overlapped.

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:41.930
Some of them didn't.

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:44.900
And then I wrote
them out into files.

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:47.390
And I actually created 2.5.

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:52.940
trillion lines in my
output, 2.5 trillion,

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:56.320
almost one petabyte of outputs.

00:15:56.320 --> 00:15:59.970
And this job took
about 7.5 hours.

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:04.680
And I was able to process
it with 10 lines of code.

00:16:07.037 --> 00:16:08.120
Back to my slides, please.

00:16:12.441 --> 00:16:13.940
So hopefully, I was
able to show you

00:16:13.940 --> 00:16:21.250
that now it's very easy
to do large scale Shuffle

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:24.260
processing with Cloud Dataflow.

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:26.320
In addition to my
100 terabyte run,

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:28.900
I also ran a few
smaller jobs just

00:16:28.900 --> 00:16:32.390
to show you the scalability
of this process.

00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:35.920
So I did a four terabyte
run, a 20 terabyte run,

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:38.500
and the 100 terabyte run.

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:42.190
With 5x data
increased every time.

00:16:42.190 --> 00:16:46.770
And the execution time
was pretty much linear,

00:16:46.770 --> 00:16:49.200
which is what you want to have.

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:52.110
You don't want to have
a quadratic escalation

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:54.030
of your duration.

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:56.250
If you can achieve
linearity in execution time,

00:16:56.250 --> 00:17:00.140
that's a very good result.

00:17:00.140 --> 00:17:02.130
What you don't see
on this slide is

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:04.829
that the resources
that you consumed

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:10.980
during this execution, what we
call Shuffle data processed,

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:12.920
also scaled linearly.

00:17:15.520 --> 00:17:18.560
If you're difference
in data is 2x,

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:21.560
you will only pay 2x
more for such a job.

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:24.250
It's another good
property of a service.

00:17:24.250 --> 00:17:26.390
You are able to
scale both duration

00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:33.030
as well as cost linearly
as your data processes.

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:35.210
Now we talked about
batch pipelines.

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:37.280
And hopefully, I
was able to show you

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:41.690
that in batch processing, being
able to do efficient shuffling

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:42.740
is important.

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:44.800
But what about
streaming processing?

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:47.340
Cloud Dataflow provides
both batch and streaming

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:48.500
capabilities.

00:17:48.500 --> 00:17:51.830
And perhaps, in streaming
you also need Shuffle,

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:52.820
you might ask.

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:55.340
The answer is, yes,
it's also very important

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:58.070
to be able to do Shuffling
in streaming pipelines.

00:17:58.070 --> 00:18:02.310
Because customers want to
join in group data elements.

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:05.540
But in addition to Shuffling,
in streaming pipeline,

00:18:05.540 --> 00:18:06.920
the other thing
that is important

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:10.940
is that you need to be able
to efficiently store state.

00:18:10.940 --> 00:18:14.870
State, as I said, relates
to windows, time windows

00:18:14.870 --> 00:18:17.930
that you create when you're
on windowing aggregations

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:20.280
on streaming data.

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:23.540
So let's go through a
similar life of a pipeline

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:27.060
but for a streaming use case.

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:30.320
So this pipeline
leads from Pub/Sub,

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:34.490
does a windowing operation by
event time, does a grouping,

00:18:34.490 --> 00:18:39.120
does a group by, does
an account aggregation,

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:41.960
and then writes into BigQuery.

00:18:41.960 --> 00:18:45.090
Now the first thing to
note here is that when

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:47.940
you submit such a
job to Dataflow.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:50.000
we're going to divide
it into three stages--

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.220
everything that comes
before the group by,

00:18:52.220 --> 00:18:54.860
everything that comes
after the group by,

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:58.410
and the actual Shuffle step.

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:01.150
And once we divide it
into three portions,

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:04.200
we're going to start thinking,
how do we scale and distributed

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:05.150
this processing?

00:19:05.150 --> 00:19:10.610
And our way of distributing
the incoming workload

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:13.310
to multiple workers
is by partitioning

00:19:13.310 --> 00:19:15.820
your data set by key.

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:18.350
If your pipeline
already has a key

00:19:18.350 --> 00:19:21.310
through maybe a group by
operation, we'll use that key.

00:19:21.310 --> 00:19:25.240
If it doesn't have a group by,
then we'll auto-generate a key

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:27.770
and partition your
data that way.

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:33.950
So in this case, for example,
we have split the key spaces

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:38.270
for the pre-Shuffle and
the post-Shuffle operations

00:19:38.270 --> 00:19:39.080
into ranges.

00:19:41.447 --> 00:19:43.030
I'm going to show
you next what we are

00:19:43.030 --> 00:19:45.464
going to do with these ranges.

00:19:45.464 --> 00:19:47.380
Well, they're going to
assign them to workers.

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:48.900
So each key range
will be assigned

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:49.950
to an individual worker.

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:56.240
Once we've done the assignment
of key ranges to workers,

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:58.080
we are going to
persist the date,

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:02.610
make specific PD volumes
responsible for storage of data

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:08.550
as they relate to these
keys and key ranges.

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:12.330
When we have to scale a
pipeline in a streaming case,

00:20:12.330 --> 00:20:14.640
we're actually going
to move an entire disk

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:16.350
from one worker to the other.

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:18.120
We're not going to
try to rearrange

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:21.020
key spaces and compress
them or reassign them.

00:20:21.020 --> 00:20:25.230
We're going to take an entire
desk, an entire persistent disk

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:29.690
and reassign it
to another worker.

00:20:29.690 --> 00:20:34.190
As you can imagine, it's another
time-intensive operation.

00:20:34.190 --> 00:20:37.430
Streaming out of scaling has
been working for many customers

00:20:37.430 --> 00:20:38.720
really well.

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:40.977
But in some cases,
it might end up

00:20:40.977 --> 00:20:42.560
being a little bit
sluggish because we

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:46.479
have to reassign disks.

00:20:46.479 --> 00:20:48.270
Now we talked about
group by, let's quickly

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:49.830
cover the windowing operation.

00:20:49.830 --> 00:20:52.560
Here, it's important to
remember, in streaming,

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:54.270
there are two important
time dimensions

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:56.400
that you want to think about.

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:59.620
The first time dimension is
your business time dimension.

00:20:59.620 --> 00:21:00.870
That's the event time.

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:02.670
This is when the
sales transaction

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:07.530
happened if you're dealing
with sales transactions.

00:21:07.530 --> 00:21:11.480
Or it might be the time when
the user clicked on a link.

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:13.590
So it's the business time.

00:21:13.590 --> 00:21:16.650
In our terminology,
we call it event time.

00:21:16.650 --> 00:21:21.040
The other important time
dimension is processing time.

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:24.310
That's the time when
the business transaction

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:26.980
enters the processing pipeline.

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:28.450
And as you can
imagine, there could

00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:31.560
be delays between
an event generated

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:34.210
by the source system
and this same event

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:38.140
entering a processing pipeline.

00:21:38.140 --> 00:21:39.940
Now many customers
want to do, they

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:42.340
don't want to deal
with processing time

00:21:42.340 --> 00:21:45.760
as a unit of analysis or
as a dimension of analysis.

00:21:45.760 --> 00:21:48.700
They actually want to
deal with event time.

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:51.130
And they want to organize
the data elements

00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:56.350
into windows of
either fixed duration,

00:21:56.350 --> 00:21:59.770
or configurable duration,
or session-based windows.

00:21:59.770 --> 00:22:05.470
But they want to group their
data elements by event time.

00:22:05.470 --> 00:22:09.130
So with this in mind, what we
have to do on the Dataflow side

00:22:09.130 --> 00:22:12.920
to allow such an
analytical processing,

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:18.040
we end up buffering
data elements on disk.

00:22:18.040 --> 00:22:21.490
Because we have to store these
elements until the window

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:24.760
closes, and we can
initiate the processing

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:27.080
of elements in the window.

00:22:27.080 --> 00:22:30.280
So in addition to
Shuffle, we also

00:22:30.280 --> 00:22:36.490
store data elements
related to windows.

00:22:36.490 --> 00:22:39.790
So we asked ourselves, can
we do something similar

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:43.390
for streaming pipelines, as
we did for the batch pipelines

00:22:43.390 --> 00:22:45.390
with Shuffle?

00:22:45.390 --> 00:22:46.530
And the answer is, yes.

00:22:46.530 --> 00:22:49.030
I'm happy to
announce that today,

00:22:49.030 --> 00:22:52.200
we made the streaming
engine available in beta

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:53.500
in two regions--

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:56.789
in US central 1
and Europe west 1.

00:22:56.789 --> 00:22:58.580
Quickly about the
benefits of the streaming

00:22:58.580 --> 00:23:03.010
engine and the architecture
of the streaming engine.

00:23:03.010 --> 00:23:05.620
Your user code-- the code
that you write that represents

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:06.760
your business logic--

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:09.580
continues to run
on worker nodes.

00:23:09.580 --> 00:23:12.160
But now, all the
streaming plumbing

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:15.430
that used to run in [INAUDIBLE]
and co-exist with your user

00:23:15.430 --> 00:23:18.580
code that used to run
on the worker nodes,

00:23:18.580 --> 00:23:23.780
it has been moved to a dedicated
service in Google's back end.

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:26.560
And it is responsible
for two things.

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:29.360
It's responsible for
windows state storage

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:31.890
as well as for
streaming Shuffle.

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:34.210
Your user code
communicates transparently.

00:23:34.210 --> 00:23:36.590
You as a developer, you
don't have to think about it

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:39.500
or worry about it.

00:23:39.500 --> 00:23:42.559
Very similarly to Dataflow
Shuffle in batch case,

00:23:42.559 --> 00:23:44.600
you only have to provide
us with a parameter that

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:48.140
tells us that you want us
to use the streaming engine.

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:51.470
So your using code transparently
communicates with us back end.

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:56.540
And we do all the shuffling and
all the state storage for you.

00:23:56.540 --> 00:24:00.870
The benefits are, we don't have
to move around disks anymore

00:24:00.870 --> 00:24:02.300
if you want to scale.

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:06.940
So out of scaling and
streaming became much better.

00:24:06.940 --> 00:24:11.020
We also can do maintenance
on our service much easier.

00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:12.210
It doesn't interrupt you.

00:24:12.210 --> 00:24:14.310
You can continue
running your pipeline.

00:24:14.310 --> 00:24:16.890
We can do maintenance and
patches on the back end

00:24:16.890 --> 00:24:20.640
without interrupting
you in most cases.

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:24.460
And we also consume less
resources on the worker nodes.

00:24:24.460 --> 00:24:27.870
So now, your user code
that implements business

00:24:27.870 --> 00:24:31.560
transactions and processes
your data element

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:34.870
has more CPU and
memory available to it.

00:24:34.870 --> 00:24:40.490
So it can produce more
and crunch more data.

00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:45.490
Here's an example that shows
you how streaming out of scaling

00:24:45.490 --> 00:24:47.800
works together with
a streaming engine.

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:54.070
And what this diagram shows
you is a incoming stream

00:24:54.070 --> 00:24:56.830
of data that ran
for about one hour

00:24:56.830 --> 00:25:01.330
and ranged in bandwidth
and throughput between one

00:25:01.330 --> 00:25:04.000
megabyte per second to
10 megabytes per second.

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:10.580
When I didn't use
the streaming engine,

00:25:10.580 --> 00:25:13.030
Cloud Dataflow
scaled my workers.

00:25:13.030 --> 00:25:15.530
And initially, it started
with three workers.

00:25:15.530 --> 00:25:18.350
And then once
Dataflow sense there's

00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:21.890
an increase in
incoming data, it's

00:25:21.890 --> 00:25:24.310
scaled the number
of workers to 15.

00:25:24.310 --> 00:25:27.920
It kept this number for awhile
and then scaled down, and then

00:25:27.920 --> 00:25:32.030
waiting for another spike in
inputs and then scaled up.

00:25:32.030 --> 00:25:35.870
Now if you compare this graph
to the graph with the streaming

00:25:35.870 --> 00:25:39.790
engine, as you can see,
we used less workers.

00:25:39.790 --> 00:25:44.760
And we were able to
scale down much faster.

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:46.380
Here are the two
graphs overlaid.

00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:49.920
And it shows you quite nicely
how the streaming engine

00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:56.470
is able to avoid scaling down
to high numbers of resources,

00:25:56.470 --> 00:25:58.860
is using less
resources, and is also

00:25:58.860 --> 00:26:05.420
more responsive to variations
in incoming data rate.

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:08.570
Of course, the best
stories about Dataflow

00:26:08.570 --> 00:26:10.020
are told by our customers.

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:11.840
And so I'm very happy
to invite today,

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:15.080
George Hilios from Two Sigma,
who will be talking to you

00:26:15.080 --> 00:26:19.075
about how Two Sigma is
using Cloud Dataflow.

00:26:19.075 --> 00:26:21.550
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:25.420
GEORGE HILIOS: Thanks, Sergei.

00:26:25.420 --> 00:26:27.280
So my name is George Hilios.

00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:29.890
I'm a vice president
at Two Sigma.

00:26:29.890 --> 00:26:33.010
Just a quick intro
into who Two Sigma is.

00:26:33.010 --> 00:26:35.380
We are a Manhattan-based
hedge fund.

00:26:35.380 --> 00:26:38.140
We take a scientific
approach to investing.

00:26:38.140 --> 00:26:42.910
We hire scientists,
mathematicians, engineers,

00:26:42.910 --> 00:26:43.780
the works.

00:26:43.780 --> 00:26:47.770
Our mission is to find
value in the world's data.

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:50.110
So in particular, I had
our engineering group

00:26:50.110 --> 00:26:53.260
that deals with
granular economic data.

00:26:53.260 --> 00:26:55.370
I'm probably getting some
squints for most of you.

00:26:55.370 --> 00:26:58.370
So let me explain what
that kind of means.

00:26:58.370 --> 00:26:59.230
Here's an example.

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:03.870
The NOAA publishes a wealth
of information publicly.

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:06.440
There's weather forecast about
all regions of the United

00:27:06.440 --> 00:27:07.590
States.

00:27:07.590 --> 00:27:09.850
And so we have a big question--

00:27:09.850 --> 00:27:13.060
can we correlate
weather activity

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:18.310
with regional economics to
predict financial outcomes?

00:27:18.310 --> 00:27:21.400
These are the types of
questions data scientists

00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:24.360
and engineers in my group ask.

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:27.574
And so this data tend
to be very large.

00:27:27.574 --> 00:27:29.490
I actually jumped slides
a little too quickly.

00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:31.110
Sorry.

00:27:31.110 --> 00:27:35.430
The NOAA data compresses
about a terabyte or so.

00:27:35.430 --> 00:27:38.190
But when you expand it and
look at all the rows of data,

00:27:38.190 --> 00:27:40.990
this is into the
hundreds of terabytes.

00:27:40.990 --> 00:27:44.250
So it's very, very large
to make sense of that.

00:27:44.250 --> 00:27:46.260
So what do we do
with all this data?

00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:55.180
We can do a lot of
things with this.

00:27:55.180 --> 00:27:59.110
We can do geospatial analysis,
aggregate billions of rows

00:27:59.110 --> 00:28:01.960
of data, terabytes
of data, ultimately,

00:28:01.960 --> 00:28:03.640
to build alpha models.

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:06.190
So this type of work
is very lucrative.

00:28:06.190 --> 00:28:08.440
It's very satisfying.

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:10.270
However, there is
a weakness there.

00:28:10.270 --> 00:28:13.270
We get a lot of our data
from third-party vendors.

00:28:13.270 --> 00:28:16.930
And so we're at risk
of bad vendor data

00:28:16.930 --> 00:28:19.190
causing bad predictions.

00:28:19.190 --> 00:28:23.570
So at Two Sigma, we take
data quality very seriously.

00:28:23.570 --> 00:28:27.490
We build in anomaly
detections into all

00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:30.010
of our pipelines
to guard ourself

00:28:30.010 --> 00:28:32.420
against these bad outcomes.

00:28:32.420 --> 00:28:35.330
Now when you're dealing
with data at this scale,

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:38.830
we have to build very
complex, high-scale systems

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:44.030
to detect these anomalies and
protect us and our investors.

00:28:44.030 --> 00:28:47.930
And so this is where
Dataflow enters.

00:28:47.930 --> 00:28:50.470
We really don't want to be
in the business of building

00:28:50.470 --> 00:28:51.520
infrastructure.

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:56.320
We'd like to focus our energy on
business logic, things nuanced

00:28:56.320 --> 00:28:58.660
to particular vendors
and the types of quirks

00:28:58.660 --> 00:29:00.550
they threw our way.

00:29:00.550 --> 00:29:04.430
But also, we're not a
particularly large team.

00:29:04.430 --> 00:29:06.100
We don't have the
resources Google

00:29:06.100 --> 00:29:09.200
tends to throw at these
infrastructure-type problems.

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:12.190
So can a team of
10 to 20 engineers

00:29:12.190 --> 00:29:15.910
deal with 100, 200 terabyte data
sets without batting an eye?

00:29:20.700 --> 00:29:22.560
Let me take a quick
little sidebar here.

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:25.710
How many of you know
what RFC-4180 is?

00:29:25.710 --> 00:29:29.670
I'll be surprised to
see any hands out there.

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:33.770
I do, and this is the
format for CSV files.

00:29:33.770 --> 00:29:36.300
These are comma
separated values.

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:38.550
It's the bread and
butter in the industry

00:29:38.550 --> 00:29:41.430
for how files are distributed.

00:29:41.430 --> 00:29:43.950
Unfortunately,
our vendors do not

00:29:43.950 --> 00:29:46.380
tend to know what that RFC is.

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:50.490
Let me show you an example
of what that really entails.

00:29:50.490 --> 00:29:52.380
And these are real examples.

00:29:52.380 --> 00:29:55.410
Obviously, change for
presentation purposes.

00:29:55.410 --> 00:29:58.410
So that comma, is that
separating two numbers,

00:29:58.410 --> 00:30:01.020
or is that 15,600?

00:30:01.020 --> 00:30:04.170
In this case, it
was actually 15,600.

00:30:04.170 --> 00:30:07.350
Good luck to the pipeline
understanding that.

00:30:07.350 --> 00:30:10.635
Within the same file, dates
represented in different ways.

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:15.030
Here is a new line character.

00:30:15.030 --> 00:30:17.190
Is that a separate
row, or is that

00:30:17.190 --> 00:30:19.680
the same string that happens
to have a new line in it.

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:21.420
A lot of these
vendors get their data

00:30:21.420 --> 00:30:24.420
from third-party
sources to them,

00:30:24.420 --> 00:30:26.400
and they're just
passing it along.

00:30:26.400 --> 00:30:28.830
So if it's unescaped
and unquoted,

00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:32.710
any off-the-shelf CSV
parser will choke on this.

00:30:32.710 --> 00:30:33.960
And here's another one.

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:37.740
We've noticed that as the
days and months go by,

00:30:37.740 --> 00:30:40.275
vendors decide to
expose new fields to us,

00:30:40.275 --> 00:30:42.520
so the schema changes.

00:30:42.520 --> 00:30:44.550
So imagine you're
a data engineer.

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:45.990
You're writing a pipeline.

00:30:45.990 --> 00:30:48.360
And you have your code
for doing aggregation.

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:50.840
Do you have a line, a
conditional in there

00:30:50.840 --> 00:30:55.680
that says, if date is
less than January 1, 2016,

00:30:55.680 --> 00:30:58.710
I only expect four rows?

00:30:58.710 --> 00:31:00.210
All your pipelines
would be littered

00:31:00.210 --> 00:31:02.100
with this vendor-specific logic.

00:31:02.100 --> 00:31:05.010
And it gets very
unwieldy very quickly.

00:31:05.010 --> 00:31:07.710
So we wanted to use
this opportunity

00:31:07.710 --> 00:31:11.520
with a tool like Dataflow to
eliminate this problem entirely

00:31:11.520 --> 00:31:13.090
and get in front of it.

00:31:13.090 --> 00:31:16.240
So let me show you an example
of what that looks like.

00:31:16.240 --> 00:31:20.550
We built some tooling, which
we called the normalizer--

00:31:20.550 --> 00:31:23.530
very creative naming we used--

00:31:23.530 --> 00:31:24.720
on top of Dataflow.

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:28.400
And the very first
step up here really

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:32.100
involves taking the
list of files and a JSON

00:31:32.100 --> 00:31:34.700
file we call the
schema as input.

00:31:34.700 --> 00:31:37.490
I'll go into details of
what that schema file looks

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:38.810
like in the next slide.

00:31:38.810 --> 00:31:42.200
But it's things like, the
first field has this name,

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:46.040
and it has this type-like
string or integer.

00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:49.370
It has hooks for Python
user-defined functions

00:31:49.370 --> 00:31:53.510
for doing custom
things, et cetera.

00:31:53.510 --> 00:31:56.221
In this second step, we
implemented a custom file

00:31:56.221 --> 00:31:56.720
source.

00:31:56.720 --> 00:31:59.360
It takes a file
as input from GCS,

00:31:59.360 --> 00:32:01.640
and it turns it into
a PCollection, which

00:32:01.640 --> 00:32:08.450
is a Apache beam slash
Dataflow concept, a PCollection

00:32:08.450 --> 00:32:12.770
of generic type, which is an
Avro object that has a schema.

00:32:12.770 --> 00:32:15.470
Again, we want to
use open source

00:32:15.470 --> 00:32:17.960
where possible to
leverage the innovations

00:32:17.960 --> 00:32:20.960
and the contributions of
the broader community.

00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:24.720
So we turn everything
into an object of a type.

00:32:24.720 --> 00:32:27.480
And much later in the
pipeline, we split.

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:28.930
The stuff that
goes one direction

00:32:28.930 --> 00:32:30.760
are all the rows
that we successfully

00:32:30.760 --> 00:32:36.730
parsed, that we applied our
logic to normalize and put

00:32:36.730 --> 00:32:38.140
everything to a schema.

00:32:38.140 --> 00:32:40.300
And we use the same
Spotify library

00:32:40.300 --> 00:32:44.230
Sergei mentioned earlier, CO,
to save it as a parquet file.

00:32:44.230 --> 00:32:45.970
There is another
functionality in there

00:32:45.970 --> 00:32:47.680
that I particularly appreciate.

00:32:47.680 --> 00:32:50.770
It's that you can say,
I want 5 million rows

00:32:50.770 --> 00:32:53.770
in every single parquet
file, which for those you

00:32:53.770 --> 00:32:56.260
have done Spark, that is
actually kind of a hard thing

00:32:56.260 --> 00:32:57.860
to do generally.

00:32:57.860 --> 00:33:01.150
So when we output our
parquet files and process

00:33:01.150 --> 00:33:03.400
them downstream, with
all the files being

00:33:03.400 --> 00:33:07.420
very evenly distributed,
you make much better use

00:33:07.420 --> 00:33:09.860
of your resources.

00:33:09.860 --> 00:33:11.800
The other output I
want to call out,

00:33:11.800 --> 00:33:16.000
because this is the hard-earned
experience that our data

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:18.820
engineering team has
built up over time.

00:33:18.820 --> 00:33:22.165
We output an extra file that
includes summary statistics.

00:33:22.165 --> 00:33:24.460
I'll also show an
example of this-- things

00:33:24.460 --> 00:33:27.400
like the total number of
rows, the number of error rows

00:33:27.400 --> 00:33:29.180
that we encountered, and so on.

00:33:29.180 --> 00:33:32.020
And even more importantly,
say you're on support,

00:33:32.020 --> 00:33:34.210
you get paged in the
middle of the night,

00:33:34.210 --> 00:33:37.120
and there's a failure,
there's a spike in errors.

00:33:37.120 --> 00:33:39.160
You'd like to have a
file you go to that

00:33:39.160 --> 00:33:42.520
contains all of the rows that
you saw that were invalid.

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:45.690
Maybe the vendor got bored
and wanted to throw a new cork

00:33:45.690 --> 00:33:47.020
your way.

00:33:47.020 --> 00:33:48.670
I'm laughing because it's true.

00:33:51.290 --> 00:33:54.250
And finally, I'm
super-proud to announce

00:33:54.250 --> 00:33:57.170
that a partnership
between us and Google,

00:33:57.170 --> 00:34:01.810
we really wanted to get parquet
loading directly into BigQuery.

00:34:01.810 --> 00:34:03.980
And Google pulled
through big time here.

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:06.490
So we not only can produce
these parquet files,

00:34:06.490 --> 00:34:08.350
but we can load
them to BigQuery.

00:34:08.350 --> 00:34:11.350
So imagine having all your
raw vendor data in BigQuery

00:34:11.350 --> 00:34:14.620
to do your research
on, to investigate

00:34:14.620 --> 00:34:16.090
failures, et cetera.

00:34:16.090 --> 00:34:19.060
And then if your aggregation
is simple or SQL-based,

00:34:19.060 --> 00:34:21.250
you can just let the Google
infrastructure handle it

00:34:21.250 --> 00:34:22.460
for you.

00:34:22.460 --> 00:34:25.330
Furthermore, since these are
all open source technologies--

00:34:25.330 --> 00:34:27.159
parquet, et cetera--

00:34:27.159 --> 00:34:28.263
the data are small.

00:34:28.263 --> 00:34:29.679
You can feed it
into [INAUDIBLE]..

00:34:29.679 --> 00:34:33.219
If the data are big and nuanced,
you can' feed it to Spark.

00:34:33.219 --> 00:34:35.780
We have one thing up front
that handles all of this.

00:34:35.780 --> 00:34:37.520
It's a big deal for us.

00:34:37.520 --> 00:34:41.181
So here's an example of a scheme
of file I talked about earlier.

00:34:41.181 --> 00:34:42.639
There's a few
different types here.

00:34:42.639 --> 00:34:46.330
That first one is a date time
in a very standard format--

00:34:46.330 --> 00:34:49.540
an integer containing
the number of seconds

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:53.679
since Unix Epoch,
January 1, 1970, and very

00:34:53.679 --> 00:34:56.510
standard convention here.

00:34:56.510 --> 00:34:58.120
Way of a string--

00:34:58.120 --> 00:35:01.510
we have a string hash that
takes its input another column.

00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:05.740
We found that if your primary
key or your foreign key

00:35:05.740 --> 00:35:08.140
is a string, that's
very compute-intensive

00:35:08.140 --> 00:35:10.240
to do joins or group bys on.

00:35:10.240 --> 00:35:13.270
And so with our standard
upfront processing,

00:35:13.270 --> 00:35:17.000
we can hash a string as input
and add a new column there.

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:18.880
So when it gets
loaded to BigQuery,

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:21.370
we have the hash as
well as the source.

00:35:21.370 --> 00:35:22.960
And then when we
process in Spark,

00:35:22.960 --> 00:35:25.420
those groups are very fast.

00:35:25.420 --> 00:35:27.670
And finally, [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:27.670 --> 00:35:29.350
Some fields are
optional, and we want

00:35:29.350 --> 00:35:33.600
to make it clear which one
should be treated as failures.

00:35:33.600 --> 00:35:36.150
So here's an example
of our output--

00:35:36.150 --> 00:35:37.240
the summary file.

00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:40.290
I'm super-proud of
the work the team did.

00:35:40.290 --> 00:35:44.160
Because this particular
vendor gave us over a trillion

00:35:44.160 --> 00:35:45.250
rows of data.

00:35:45.250 --> 00:35:47.490
And this is one of our
days worth of delivery.

00:35:47.490 --> 00:35:51.780
We were successfully able to
parse 916 million records.

00:35:51.780 --> 00:35:53.880
And we did this in a matter
of hours on Dataflow.

00:35:53.880 --> 00:35:56.610
It scaled very linearly.

00:35:56.610 --> 00:35:58.690
There's 185 parquet files.

00:35:58.690 --> 00:36:01.110
You can kind of see
behind the circle,

00:36:01.110 --> 00:36:02.490
they're very evenly-sized.

00:36:02.490 --> 00:36:04.770
This is great for
Spark processing.

00:36:04.770 --> 00:36:07.110
And the errors rows
is 0, because we

00:36:07.110 --> 00:36:08.790
had no failures that day--

00:36:08.790 --> 00:36:11.080
very powerful.

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:13.550
So let's tie this all together.

00:36:13.550 --> 00:36:15.010
We have the data
from the vendor.

00:36:15.010 --> 00:36:19.870
They give it to us FTPS,
3GCS, carrier pigeon,

00:36:19.870 --> 00:36:22.330
whatever they want to do.

00:36:22.330 --> 00:36:27.590
We first store it in GCS and
then process it in Dataflow.

00:36:27.590 --> 00:36:29.270
This is where we do
our normalization,

00:36:29.270 --> 00:36:33.470
turn it into parquet, and
output our summary statistics.

00:36:33.470 --> 00:36:35.180
We then run our
ingestion checks.

00:36:35.180 --> 00:36:37.160
Like I mentioned
earlier, we care deeply

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:39.185
about data quality in Two Sigma.

00:36:39.185 --> 00:36:44.090
So we do things like fail if we
have a spike or a sharp decline

00:36:44.090 --> 00:36:47.630
in the number of rows processed
or the number of errors

00:36:47.630 --> 00:36:48.590
encountered.

00:36:48.590 --> 00:36:50.240
Ideally, we have zero errors.

00:36:50.240 --> 00:36:52.520
But sometimes, if you're
processing a billion rows,

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:54.980
you sort of shrug
at five failed rows.

00:36:54.980 --> 00:36:57.900
That's just the reality
of the situation.

00:36:57.900 --> 00:37:00.230
And last but not least,
we load to BigQuery

00:37:00.230 --> 00:37:05.130
for analysis, research,
and further processing.

00:37:05.130 --> 00:37:08.120
So I do want to highlight some
of the high-level benefits we

00:37:08.120 --> 00:37:09.080
get here.

00:37:09.080 --> 00:37:11.750
A small team like
ours can process

00:37:11.750 --> 00:37:14.120
a ton of data with
very little effort.

00:37:14.120 --> 00:37:18.110
And that trillion row data set
example I told you earlier,

00:37:18.110 --> 00:37:19.910
we tried it in Spark previously.

00:37:19.910 --> 00:37:21.800
And it took 1,000
workers two weeks

00:37:21.800 --> 00:37:23.690
to crank through everything.

00:37:23.690 --> 00:37:25.310
And this is a pretty
simple algorithm,

00:37:25.310 --> 00:37:28.030
just parsing strings and
turning them into parquet.

00:37:28.030 --> 00:37:32.467
In Dataflow, we did the whole
data set in one to two days.

00:37:32.467 --> 00:37:34.300
One other benefit is
we don't have to manage

00:37:34.300 --> 00:37:35.860
any of the infrastructure.

00:37:35.860 --> 00:37:39.282
We just write our beam
pipeline and send it to Google.

00:37:39.282 --> 00:37:41.490
And there's one more thing
I want to highlight there.

00:37:41.490 --> 00:37:44.260
Sergei talked earlier
about Cloud Shuffle.

00:37:44.260 --> 00:37:47.020
We found we should
just turn it on.

00:37:47.020 --> 00:37:49.960
So either the data
set is small enough

00:37:49.960 --> 00:37:51.770
to where Shuffle
is not invoked--

00:37:51.770 --> 00:37:53.500
you can see on the
UI, there's a number

00:37:53.500 --> 00:37:56.680
of bytes shuffled-- if
this number is small,

00:37:56.680 --> 00:37:59.300
you don't really pay
much of anything for it.

00:37:59.300 --> 00:38:03.130
But when that number is big,
and your data set is very large,

00:38:03.130 --> 00:38:07.960
we've seen pipelines
take much less time

00:38:07.960 --> 00:38:11.650
to run, which saves you on
compute and storage costs.

00:38:11.650 --> 00:38:12.600
That's it.

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:13.886
Thank you.

00:38:13.886 --> 00:38:16.351
[APPLAUSE]

00:38:17.830 --> 00:38:20.016
SERGEI SOKOLENKO: But
let's wrap this up.

00:38:20.016 --> 00:38:22.320
Hopefully, you were
able to see how

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:27.510
having a separate distributed
in-memory processing

00:38:27.510 --> 00:38:32.610
mechanism for Shuffle improves
the efficiency of separating

00:38:32.610 --> 00:38:33.690
compute from storage.

00:38:33.690 --> 00:38:36.930
That was key for both batch
and streaming pipelines.

00:38:36.930 --> 00:38:39.350
We have a new service
available for you today,

00:38:39.350 --> 00:38:40.370
Dataflow Shuffle--

00:38:40.370 --> 00:38:42.250
a new component, sorry,
not a new service.

00:38:42.250 --> 00:38:44.220
We are not launching
a new service.

00:38:44.220 --> 00:38:46.890
So for batch pipelines,
we have a new option

00:38:46.890 --> 00:38:48.570
for you, Dataflow
Shuffle, that makes

00:38:48.570 --> 00:38:50.510
your pipelines run faster.

00:38:50.510 --> 00:38:53.580
And you are now able to
process much larger data sets

00:38:53.580 --> 00:38:55.760
and shuffle much
larger data sets.

00:38:55.760 --> 00:38:58.560
For the streaming pipelines,
we have the streaming engine.

00:38:58.560 --> 00:39:03.090
And it improves the scalability
of your streaming pipelines.

00:39:03.090 --> 00:39:07.410
Both of these features are now
available in two GCP regions--

00:39:07.410 --> 00:39:10.747
in US central one,
and Europe west one.

00:39:10.747 --> 00:39:11.580
Thank you very much.

00:39:11.580 --> 00:39:13.163
And we will be happy
to take questions

00:39:13.163 --> 00:39:16.510
either over microphone or
later on after the session.

00:39:16.510 --> 00:39:20.235
[MUSIC PLAYING]

